PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The accredited investor (AI) definition is set out in NI 45-106 (Ontario) as of January 2021. An entity such as a limited partnership qualifies as an AI if (i) it has net assets of at least $5 million or (ii) every owner of an interest (direct, indirect or beneficial) is itself an AI.

Option A supplies no financial statements or asset values for the limited partnership itself, so test (i) cannot be satisfied. Under test (ii), the three limited partners are AIs, but the sole general partner is a holding company whose qualification rests on either its own net assets or the AI status of its shareholders. Nothing in the facts demonstrates that the holding company meets the $5 million net-asset test on a stand-alone basis, nor is there any representation that the holding company itself satisfies the “all owners are AIs” test once the circular ownership is examined. Consequently the LP fails both prongs and is not an AI. All other choices meet at least one of the enumerated AI tests on the facts given.}

